1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,791
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,791 --> 00:00:21,279
Tonight we travel to Edo, the great city that would one day be called Tokyo, on a cool autumn evening about two hundred years ago.

3
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:23,618
Nothing dramatic will happen.

4
00:00:23,618 --> 00:00:33,218
We will only listen to the temple bells counting the hours, and watch a vast wooden city put out its lamps, one by one.

5
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:38,129
Settle yourself the way you like to sleep.

6
00:00:38,129 --> 00:00:41,742
Let your shoulders sink and your hands go loose.

7
00:00:41,742 --> 00:00:52,881
Take one slow breath in, through the nose, and imagine the air is cool and faintly sweet, with a little woodsmoke in it, and the smell of the river.

8
00:00:52,881 --> 00:00:56,493
Then let the breath go, slowly, all the way out.

9
00:00:58,243 --> 00:01:05,386
You can follow along, or you can simply let the words drift past you like boats on dark water.

10
00:01:05,386 --> 00:01:09,185
There is nothing to learn and nothing to remember.

11
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:17,315
If you fall asleep before the last bell, the night will carry on quietly without you, exactly as it should.

12
00:01:27,066 --> 00:01:37,228
We begin on a riverbank in the very heart of the city, beside a long wooden bridge that rises in a gentle arch over the water.

13
00:01:37,228 --> 00:01:40,455
This is Nihonbashi, the Bridge of Japan.

14
00:01:40,455 --> 00:01:56,506
The first one was built here in 1603, the year the city became the seat of the shogun, and it has been rebuilt more than once since then, always in wood, always with the same soft hump in the middle.

15
00:01:58,256 --> 00:02:02,361
Every great road in the country begins on this bridge.

16
00:02:02,361 --> 00:02:14,828
Five highways run out from here, along the coast, over the mountains, and up into the north, and every distance in Edo is counted from the planks beneath your feet.

17
00:02:14,828 --> 00:02:25,776
A traveller setting off for distant Kyoto would start walking right here, with a straw hat and a small bundle, and a journey of many days ahead.

18
00:02:27,526 --> 00:02:38,386
From the high middle of the bridge, if you turn to the west, you can see the dark roofs of the shogun's castle rising above its walls and trees.

19
00:02:38,386 --> 00:02:53,168
And far beyond it, floating over the hills, there is the pale cone of Mount Fuji, still holding a little rose-coloured light on its snowy top, long after the streets below have fallen into shadow.

20
00:02:54,918 --> 00:02:58,030
Now a sound comes across the rooftops.

21
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:06,873
Three slow strokes on a great bronze bell, spaced well apart, to tell the listeners that the hour is coming.

22
00:03:06,873 --> 00:03:12,358
Then the hour itself, rung out stroke after stroke, deep and round.

23
00:03:12,358 --> 00:03:18,745
This is the bell at Hongokucho, close by, the oldest of the time bells in Edo.

24
00:03:18,745 --> 00:03:20,710
It is announcing sunset.

25
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:26,847
In Edo, the hours are not the same length all year round.

26
00:03:26,847 --> 00:03:34,389
The day is split into six parts from dawn to dusk, and the night into six parts from dusk to dawn.

27
00:03:34,389 --> 00:03:46,780
So in summer the daylight hours are long and the night hours short, and now, in autumn, the night hours are slowly stretching out, a little longer every evening.

28
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:51,796
The ringing does not stop with this one bell.

29
00:03:51,796 --> 00:04:00,289
Far off, another temple takes up the hour, and then another beyond that, each placed just within hearing of the last.

30
00:04:00,289 --> 00:04:03,772
Nine of them, in a great ring around the castle.

31
00:04:03,772 --> 00:04:13,789
You can hear the sound travel away from you across the city, getting softer and softer, until you are not sure whether you hear it at all.

32
00:04:15,539 --> 00:04:21,620
Beside the bridge, the fish market that was so loud this morning is quiet now.

33
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:29,650
The wooden stalls are rinsed and empty, the baskets stacked and drying, the buckets turned upside down.

34
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:35,342
A few gulls stand on the rail of a moored boat, looking out at the water.

35
00:04:35,342 --> 00:04:39,240
One of them tucks its head away and goes to sleep.

36
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:53,661
On the far bank, a long row of white-walled storehouses stands along the river, their thick plaster walls glowing faintly in the dusk, their heavy doors shut for the night.

37
00:04:53,661 --> 00:05:06,185
And on the bridge itself, people are hurrying home now, wooden sandals clicking on the boards, and here and there someone unfolds a paper lantern and lights it with care.

38
00:05:07,935 --> 00:05:21,738
Along the riverbank, the cargo boats are tied up for the night, low and broad, loaded with straw bales of rice, barrels of soy sauce, and bundles of firewood brought in from the countryside.

39
00:05:21,738 --> 00:05:29,439
Their boatmen sit on the decks with their long thin pipes, a thread of tobacco smoke rising from each one.

40
00:05:29,439 --> 00:05:37,140
One of them taps his pipe out against the side of the boat, three soft knocks, and tucks it into his sash.

41
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:48,020
Above the city, the sky turns from rose to violet to a deep, clear blue, and the first stars come out over the castle.

42
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:56,685
A flight of wild geese passes high overhead, heading south in a long, loose line, calling softly to one another.

43
00:05:56,685 --> 00:06:06,666
In Edo, the coming of the geese is one of the signs of autumn, and a few people pause on the bridge to look up and watch them go.

44
00:06:16,416 --> 00:06:23,766
Let us follow the water downstream, to where the narrow canal opens into the great Sumida river.

45
00:06:23,766 --> 00:06:34,025
Here the river is wide and slow, and the moon has come up over the eastern bank, laying a trembling path of silver across the surface.

46
00:06:34,025 --> 00:06:36,399
The air is cooler on the water.

47
00:06:36,399 --> 00:06:39,691
You might pull your collar a little closer.

48
00:06:41,441 --> 00:06:44,732
A pleasure boat is coming down the river.

49
00:06:44,732 --> 00:06:55,730
It has a low wooden roof and walls of paper screens, and the lamps inside make the whole cabin glow like a lantern floating on the water.

50
00:06:55,730 --> 00:07:06,486
At the stern, a boatman leans on a long pole, pushes, lifts it, and leans again, in an easy rhythm he has repeated ten thousand times.

51
00:07:08,236 --> 00:07:17,637
Inside, a few friends are sitting on cushions around a low table, with small cups of warm rice wine and a dish of grilled fish.

52
00:07:17,637 --> 00:07:19,561
They are not talking much.

53
00:07:19,561 --> 00:07:26,963
One of them has slid back a screen to look at the moon, and the others have turned to look with him.

54
00:07:26,963 --> 00:07:35,031
On autumn nights like this one, people in Edo hire boats for exactly this: to drift, and to look at the moon.

55
00:07:36,781 --> 00:07:46,587
Further down, a long wooden bridge crosses the river on dozens of thin legs, so long that its far end disappears into the haze.

56
00:07:46,587 --> 00:07:52,841
A few lanterns move slowly along it, like the slow sparks of people walking home.

57
00:07:52,841 --> 00:08:02,338
In summer this stretch of river would be crowded with boats, but tonight there are only a handful, spread out and drifting.

58
00:08:04,088 --> 00:08:14,747
The story goes that, more than a thousand years ago, two brothers were fishing in this river when they drew up something heavy in their net.

59
00:08:14,747 --> 00:08:22,133
It was not a fish, but a small golden statue of the gentle Kannon, the bodhisattva of compassion.

60
00:08:22,133 --> 00:08:27,539
They took it home, and a temple grew up around it, upstream at Asakusa.

61
00:08:27,539 --> 00:08:30,584
We will visit that temple later tonight.

62
00:08:32,334 --> 00:08:40,284
On the near bank, a willow leans out over the water, its long thin branches hanging almost to the surface.

63
00:08:40,284 --> 00:08:48,834
A breeze moves through it, and the strands sway together, slowly, one way and then back, like a curtain breathing.

64
00:08:48,834 --> 00:08:56,484
Under the willow, a small wooden stall has set up for the night, with a lantern hanging from its roof.

65
00:08:58,233 --> 00:09:06,159
From the corner of the stall hangs a little bell of iron, a wind chime with a strip of paper beneath it.

66
00:09:06,159 --> 00:09:13,628
When the breeze comes, the paper turns and the chime gives one small clear note, and then another.

67
00:09:13,628 --> 00:09:23,535
It is a sign that noodles are being served here, and anyone on the riverbank who hears that sound knows that hot soup is close by.

68
00:09:25,285 --> 00:09:36,113
The pleasure boat has drifted on downstream now, its glow growing smaller, its reflection stretching long and gold across the water behind it.

69
00:09:36,113 --> 00:09:40,688
The boatman's pole knocks once against the side of the hull.

70
00:09:40,688 --> 00:09:50,906
Then there is only the quiet lap of the river against the stone of the bank, and the willow, and the small chime, turning in the wind.

71
00:09:52,656 --> 00:09:57,977
The noodle seller is a young man with a cotton cloth tied around his head.

72
00:09:57,977 --> 00:10:11,421
He carries his whole kitchen on a pole across his shoulders: at one end a little charcoal stove with a pot of broth, at the other a stack of drawers holding noodles, bowls and chopsticks.

73
00:10:11,421 --> 00:10:13,794
A bowl costs sixteen small coins.

74
00:10:13,794 --> 00:10:22,565
The noodles are buckwheat, thin and grey-brown, served in a dark, savoury broth with a scattering of chopped spring onion.

75
00:10:24,315 --> 00:10:29,786
A ferryman ties up his little boat at the bank and comes over for a bowl.

76
00:10:29,786 --> 00:10:39,903
He sits on an upturned bucket, holds the bowl close to his face, and eats slowly, the steam drifting around his cheeks in the cool air.

77
00:10:39,903 --> 00:10:50,619
Edo is full of working men who live alone, far from their home villages, and small stalls like this one feed many of them, late into the night.

78
00:11:00,369 --> 00:11:05,763
We turn away from the river now, and walk inland, into the merchant quarter.

79
00:11:05,763 --> 00:11:13,287
The streets here are lined with wooden shophouses, two storeys high, with dark tiled roofs and deep eaves.

80
00:11:13,287 --> 00:11:25,991
The fronts of the shops are made of wooden lattice, and through the slats you can see the warm glow of lamps inside, where the families who keep the shops are finishing their day.

81
00:11:27,741 --> 00:11:37,608
Each shop has a curtain hanging over its door, a short length of dyed cloth split into panels, so you can push through it with your hand.

82
00:11:37,608 --> 00:11:42,649
It carries the name of the shop, or its sign, in white on deep indigo.

83
00:11:42,649 --> 00:11:45,962
All day the curtains hang out over the street.

84
00:11:45,962 --> 00:11:50,860
Now, one by one, the shopkeepers are reaching up and taking them in.

85
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:55,325
When the curtain comes down, the shop is closed for the night.

86
00:11:57,075 --> 00:12:06,122
Under the eaves of a lantern maker's shop, a dozen round paper lanterns are hanging on hooks, lit to show what he sells.

87
00:12:06,122 --> 00:12:20,145
Each one is made of thin split bamboo wound round and round in a spiral, with paper pasted over it, so that when it is not needed, it folds down flat into its own base like a closed fan.

88
00:12:20,145 --> 00:12:25,875
They sway a little in the breeze, all slightly out of time with one another.

89
00:12:27,625 --> 00:12:32,188
Inside the shop, the lantern maker is cleaning his brushes.

90
00:12:32,188 --> 00:12:42,861
He has spent the afternoon painting a family crest onto a new lantern, a circle with three leaves inside, in black ink on the cream paper.

91
00:12:42,861 --> 00:12:45,877
It is drying now on a rack by the wall.

92
00:12:45,877 --> 00:12:52,606
Tomorrow someone will come to collect it, and carry it home through these same streets.

93
00:12:54,356 --> 00:12:59,753
Next door, a seller of tea is folding the cloths that covered his jars.

94
00:12:59,753 --> 00:13:15,489
Across the lane, a woman in a plain cotton robe is sweeping the earth in front of her doorway with a short broom, in neat, even strokes, and then she sprinkles a little water from a ladle to settle the dust.

95
00:13:15,489 --> 00:13:21,343
It is something people do here every evening, a small courtesy to the street.

96
00:13:23,093 --> 00:13:34,261
Halfway down the lane, a tall ladder rises above the rooftops, with a little railed platform at the top and a small bronze bell hanging from a post.

97
00:13:34,261 --> 00:13:36,072
It is a fire watchtower.

98
00:13:36,072 --> 00:13:43,995
Every few blocks in Edo there is one like it, so that someone can climb up and look out across the roofs.

99
00:13:43,995 --> 00:13:48,522
Tonight the air is calm and still, and the bell hangs quiet.

100
00:13:50,272 --> 00:13:58,170
At the end of the lane stands a wooden gate, with two heavy doors and a little wicket door set into one of them.

101
00:13:58,170 --> 00:14:07,337
Edo is divided into thousands of these small neighbourhoods, each with its own gate, and late in the evening the gates are closed.

102
00:14:07,337 --> 00:14:17,280
After that, a late traveller must knock at the wicket, and the old gatekeeper in his little hut will look out, and nod, and let them through.

103
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:24,831
For now, the gate is still open, and people pass through it on their way home.

104
00:14:24,831 --> 00:14:28,401
A carpenter with a box of tools on his shoulder.

105
00:14:28,401 --> 00:14:34,351
Two children running ahead of their grandmother, who calls to them to slow down.

106
00:14:34,351 --> 00:14:39,780
A young man carrying a paper-wrapped parcel very carefully in both hands.

107
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:46,102
Each of them is followed by a small circle of lantern light, moving along the ground.

108
00:14:47,852 --> 00:15:02,006
In a doorway, a mother is sitting on the step with a baby tied on her back, rocking gently from side to side and humming the same few notes of a lullaby, over and over, very quietly.

109
00:15:02,006 --> 00:15:04,728
The baby's eyes are already closed.

110
00:15:04,728 --> 00:15:12,193
A neighbour passing by stops to look, smiles, and walks on without a word, so as not to wake it.

111
00:15:13,943 --> 00:15:23,298
Up on the second floor of one of the shops, a lamp moves behind a paper window as someone carries it from one room to another.

112
00:15:23,298 --> 00:15:28,050
A shadow passes across the paper, stoops, and straightens again.

113
00:15:28,050 --> 00:15:40,077
It is the shop's young apprentice, laying out his bedding in the little upper room where he sleeps, tired from a long day of carrying parcels and sweeping floors.

114
00:15:49,827 --> 00:15:57,068
Above the roofs at the far end of the lane, a thin column of steam is rising into the moonlight.

115
00:15:57,068 --> 00:16:01,368
It comes from the chimney of the neighbourhood bathhouse.

116
00:16:01,368 --> 00:16:12,909
Most of the small homes in Edo have no bath of their own, so in the evening, after work, half the street comes here to wash and soak and talk before bed.

117
00:16:14,659 --> 00:16:21,586
A curtain hangs over the bathhouse door, with the character for hot water dyed into it.

118
00:16:21,586 --> 00:16:27,557
Inside, at a raised wooden counter, an attendant sits collecting the coins.

119
00:16:27,557 --> 00:16:35,756
The price of a bath is a handful of small copper coins, about ten of them, less than a bowl of noodles.

120
00:16:35,756 --> 00:16:37,588
A child pays even less.

121
00:16:37,588 --> 00:16:41,966
It is one of the small, reliable pleasures of the city.

122
00:16:43,716 --> 00:16:54,083
You leave your sandals at the door, undress in a wooden-floored room with shelves and baskets for your clothes, and step into the washing area.

123
00:16:54,083 --> 00:16:58,941
The floor is wooden boards, slightly sloped so the water runs away.

124
00:16:58,941 --> 00:17:10,105
You scoop warm water from a tub with a little wooden bucket and pour it over your shoulders, once, twice, and the cool of the evening washes away with it.

125
00:17:11,855 --> 00:17:21,642
The bath itself is kept behind a low partition, with an opening so low that you must stoop almost double to pass through it.

126
00:17:21,642 --> 00:17:24,563
This keeps the heat and steam inside.

127
00:17:24,563 --> 00:17:37,349
Beyond it, the room is warm and dim and full of mist, so thick that you can barely see the faces of the other bathers, only shapes moving slowly in the lamplight.

128
00:17:39,099 --> 00:17:47,796
You lower yourself into the hot water, very slowly, a little at a time, and let it rise around you up to your chin.

129
00:17:47,796 --> 00:17:53,015
The heat goes into your shoulders, your back, the backs of your legs.

130
00:17:53,015 --> 00:17:56,645
Somebody nearby lets out a long, contented sigh.

131
00:17:56,645 --> 00:18:01,637
Somebody else is humming a few lines of a song under their breath.

132
00:18:01,637 --> 00:18:03,376
Otherwise, it is quiet.

133
00:18:05,126 --> 00:18:16,470
Afterwards, wrapped in a light cotton robe, you sit for a while on a bench in the cool air near the door, letting the warmth slowly leave your skin.

134
00:18:16,470 --> 00:18:23,216
An old man beside you is fanning himself with a round paper fan, slowly, back and forth.

135
00:18:23,216 --> 00:18:25,438
Neither of you says anything.

136
00:18:25,438 --> 00:18:26,971
There is no need to.

137
00:18:28,721 --> 00:18:40,111
When you step out into the lane again, the night air feels fresh and clean, and your body feels heavy and loose, the way it does after a long hot soak.

138
00:18:40,111 --> 00:18:43,581
Your sandals click softly on the packed earth.

139
00:18:43,581 --> 00:18:54,065
The steam rises behind you from the chimney, white against the stars, thinner now as the fires under the great tub are allowed to burn low.

140
00:18:55,816 --> 00:19:12,031
On the walls of the changing room hang wooden boards painted with notices: a shop that sells combs and hairpins, a pharmacist with a well-known remedy for coughs, a new play opening soon at one of the theatres.

141
00:19:12,031 --> 00:19:15,969
People read them as they dress, and talk them over.

142
00:19:15,969 --> 00:19:23,613
The bathhouse is where the news of the neighbourhood is passed quietly from one person to the next.

143
00:19:25,363 --> 00:19:42,427
In many bathhouses, a narrow wooden stair leads to a room upstairs, where men can sit on the tatami after their bath, sip a cup of tea, and play a slow game of go, the black and white stones clicking softly on the board.

144
00:19:42,427 --> 00:19:51,347
From the window they can look down on the lane below and watch the lanterns passing, one by one, on their way home.

145
00:20:01,097 --> 00:20:12,125
Let us travel now, in the easy way that happens in dreams, upstream to Asakusa, to the temple of Kannon whose statue was found in the river.

146
00:20:12,125 --> 00:20:18,584
The great gate is shut and the stalls along the approach are closed and shuttered.

147
00:20:18,584 --> 00:20:26,697
The wide gravel courtyard is empty, except for the moonlight, and a few pigeons asleep under the eaves.

148
00:20:28,447 --> 00:20:34,235
Beside the main hall, the five-storey pagoda rises into the night sky.

149
00:20:34,235 --> 00:20:44,487
It has five roofs, one above the other, each a little smaller than the one below, each curving gently upward at its corners.

150
00:20:44,487 --> 00:20:50,523
On top stands a tall bronze spire with nine rings, pointing at the stars.

151
00:20:50,523 --> 00:20:59,039
This pagoda was raised in 1648, with help from the shogun himself, after fire had taken the one before.

152
00:21:00,789 --> 00:21:10,179
Along the paths, stone lanterns stand on their thick stone legs, each with a small room at the top where a flame can be set.

153
00:21:10,179 --> 00:21:17,222
A monk has lit a few of them, and their little square windows glow a soft orange in the dark.

154
00:21:17,222 --> 00:21:24,341
Around their bases, moss has grown in the cracks between the stones, deep green even at night.

155
00:21:26,091 --> 00:21:36,871
In its open belfry, the temple bell hangs still and heavy, a great bronze shape with a wooden beam beside it, hung on ropes, for striking.

156
00:21:36,871 --> 00:21:38,512
Soon it will be time.

157
00:21:38,512 --> 00:21:52,651
A monk in a dark robe climbs the few steps to the platform, takes the rope in both hands, and waits, listening to the bell before his, far off across the roofs, finishing its count.

158
00:21:54,401 --> 00:21:57,791
Then he draws back the beam and lets it swing.

159
00:21:57,791 --> 00:22:08,845
The bell speaks, low and long, and the sound does not end so much as fade, very slowly, humming in the air and in the wooden boards beneath your feet.

160
00:22:08,845 --> 00:22:10,467
Three warning strokes.

161
00:22:10,467 --> 00:22:11,499
Then the hour.

162
00:22:11,499 --> 00:22:19,531
It is the fourth hour of the night, and across the city, the neighbourhood gates are being closed and barred.

163
00:22:21,281 --> 00:22:26,960
Near the temple, a seller of singing insects is packing up for the night.

164
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:34,116
His stall is a wooden frame hung with dozens of tiny bamboo cages, no bigger than your hand.

165
00:22:34,116 --> 00:22:40,339
Inside them are bell crickets and pine crickets, and each kind has its own song.

166
00:22:40,339 --> 00:22:46,561
All evening, people have stopped here to listen, and to choose one to take home.

167
00:22:48,311 --> 00:22:53,605
Even now, as he wraps the cages in a cloth, the crickets keep singing.

168
00:22:53,605 --> 00:23:00,712
The bell crickets make a sound like a tiny silver bell being shaken, very fast, over and over.

169
00:23:00,712 --> 00:23:04,871
The pine crickets answer with a softer, two-note chirp.

170
00:23:04,871 --> 00:23:16,365
In autumn, people in Edo walk out to the edges of the city just to sit in the grass and listen to them, the way others go to look at blossoms in spring.

171
00:23:18,115 --> 00:23:23,006
By the temple wall, a maple tree has turned the colour of embers.

172
00:23:23,006 --> 00:23:32,940
Every now and then a single leaf lets go and spins slowly down through the lantern light, and settles on the gravel without a sound.

173
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:44,077
Beside it, a clump of tall pampas grass has opened its silver plumes, and they lean together in the moonlight, nodding, as if they were half asleep.

174
00:23:45,827 --> 00:23:58,186
The moon is high now, and its light falls across the courtyard so brightly that the pagoda lays a long shadow over the gravel, five roofs stacked in dark silhouette.

175
00:23:58,186 --> 00:24:12,117
A few weeks ago, on the night of the harvest moon, families all over the city set out little white rice dumplings and sprays of pampas grass on their verandas as an offering to the moon.

176
00:24:12,117 --> 00:24:16,910
In some houses, the grass is still standing in its vase tonight.

177
00:24:18,660 --> 00:24:27,940
Down by the river, below the temple, the water is black and very still, and the moon lies on it in a long, broken line.

178
00:24:27,940 --> 00:24:36,830
A heron stands in the shallows on one leg, perfectly motionless, the way it has been standing for an hour or more.

179
00:24:36,830 --> 00:24:38,546
It is not fishing now.

180
00:24:38,546 --> 00:24:44,239
It is simply resting, the way you are resting, with nowhere at all to be.

181
00:24:53,989 --> 00:25:06,411
Now we go home, back through the quiet streets, through the wicket of the ward gate, past the nodding gatekeeper, and down a narrow alley between long wooden row houses.

182
00:25:06,411 --> 00:25:11,997
Each house is a single room with a little earth-floored kitchen by the door.

183
00:25:11,997 --> 00:25:18,906
You slide the door open on its wooden groove, step up out of your sandals, and you are inside.

184
00:25:20,656 --> 00:25:27,931
The floor is covered with tatami mats, thick and firm and woven from rush, with dark cloth edges.

185
00:25:27,931 --> 00:25:30,856
They smell faintly of dry summer grass.

186
00:25:30,856 --> 00:25:47,429
Along one side of the room, the windows are covered with paper screens, and the moonlight shines through them, so that the whole wall glows a soft pale blue, with the shadow of a pine branch from the alley laid across it.

187
00:25:49,179 --> 00:26:01,092
In the corner stands a paper floor lamp, a tall wooden frame with paper stretched around it and a small dish of oil inside, with a wick of pith resting in it.

188
00:26:01,092 --> 00:26:06,595
Better-off families burn rapeseed oil, which gives a clear, steady light.

189
00:26:06,595 --> 00:26:11,948
Poorer ones burn cheaper sardine oil, which smells a little of the sea.

190
00:26:11,948 --> 00:26:16,849
Tonight the lamp is turned low, and its light is warm and gentle.

191
00:26:18,599 --> 00:26:30,121
In the middle of the room sits a round brazier of glazed clay, half full of fine ash, with a few pieces of charcoal glowing red in the centre.

192
00:26:30,121 --> 00:26:37,505
An iron kettle rests on a trivet above them, and a thin curl of steam rises from its spout.

193
00:26:37,505 --> 00:26:45,457
The water inside is just beginning to murmur, making a low, soft sound like wind in distant pines.

194
00:26:47,207 --> 00:26:55,162
You pour a little of the hot water into a pot of roasted tea, and then into a small cup with no handle.

195
00:26:55,162 --> 00:26:57,865
Hold it in both hands for a moment.

196
00:26:57,865 --> 00:27:02,653
Feel the warmth go into your palms and up through your wrists.

197
00:27:02,653 --> 00:27:05,819
The tea is brown and smoky and very mild.

198
00:27:05,819 --> 00:27:10,375
You drink it slowly, and the warmth spreads out inside you.

199
00:27:12,125 --> 00:27:20,444
On a low shelf by the window, the little bamboo cage from the temple sits beside a vase of pampas grass.

200
00:27:20,444 --> 00:27:31,161
The bell cricket inside has settled in, and every so often it begins to sing again, a tiny shimmering sound that fills the whole room.

201
00:27:31,161 --> 00:27:36,040
Then it stops, as though listening, and then it begins again.

202
00:27:37,790 --> 00:27:40,630
Now it is time to lay out the bedding.

203
00:27:40,630 --> 00:27:48,626
From a cupboard behind a sliding door, you lift down the thick padded mattress and unfold it on the tatami.

204
00:27:48,626 --> 00:27:53,484
Over it goes a heavy quilt, soft and quilted with cotton wadding.

205
00:27:53,484 --> 00:28:02,750
The pillow is small and firm, filled with buckwheat husks that shift and settle under your head with a quiet, sandy whisper.

206
00:28:04,500 --> 00:28:13,371
You turn the lamp down until only a small bead of flame remains, glowing through the paper like a little moon of its own.

207
00:28:13,371 --> 00:28:14,030
Lie back.

208
00:28:14,030 --> 00:28:20,555
Feel the firm tatami beneath the mattress, and the weight of the quilt settling over you.

209
00:28:20,555 --> 00:28:28,692
Through the thin walls, you can hear the neighbours moving about, then a murmur of voices, then nothing at all.

210
00:28:30,442 --> 00:28:38,690
On the wall hangs a single scroll, with a heron standing among reeds, painted in a few strokes of grey ink.

211
00:28:38,690 --> 00:28:50,946
Below it, on a narrow shelf, there is a small household altar with a bowl of rice, a cup of water and a sprig of evergreen, set out for the family's ancestors.

212
00:28:50,946 --> 00:28:58,346
A thin stick of incense was lit there earlier, and its faint sweet smell still hangs in the air.

213
00:29:00,096 --> 00:29:04,813
The house is small, but it holds everything that is needed.

214
00:29:04,813 --> 00:29:08,491
A chest with the family's robes folded inside.

215
00:29:08,491 --> 00:29:11,370
A few pots and bowls in the kitchen.

216
00:29:11,370 --> 00:29:13,768
A wooden tub for rinsing rice.

217
00:29:13,768 --> 00:29:22,723
By day, with the bedding put away, this same room is a workshop, a dining room, and a place to sit with friends.

218
00:29:22,723 --> 00:29:27,680
At night it becomes a bedroom, simply by unfolding the quilts.

219
00:29:37,430 --> 00:29:45,527
Out in the lane, under the moon, an old man is walking slowly from one end of the neighbourhood to the other.

220
00:29:45,527 --> 00:29:50,207
He wears a short padded jacket and carries a lantern on a pole.

221
00:29:50,207 --> 00:29:56,000
In his other hand he holds two blocks of hard wood, tied together with a cord.

222
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:02,091
He is the night watchman, and he has walked this route every night for many years.

223
00:30:03,841 --> 00:30:09,461
Every so often he stops, raises the blocks, and claps them together twice.

224
00:30:09,461 --> 00:30:10,449
Clack, clack.

225
00:30:10,449 --> 00:30:16,676
The sound is sharp and clean, and it carries a long way between the wooden houses.

226
00:30:16,676 --> 00:30:26,397
Then he calls out, in a long, sing-song voice, a gentle reminder to the sleeping street to take care with their fires and lamps.

227
00:30:26,397 --> 00:30:29,207
Then two more claps, and he walks on.

228
00:30:30,957 --> 00:30:35,511
In a city built of wood and paper, this is a comfort to hear.

229
00:30:35,511 --> 00:30:40,514
It means that someone is awake, and watching, and that all is well.

230
00:30:40,514 --> 00:30:45,367
Mothers half wake, hear the clappers, and close their eyes again.

231
00:30:45,367 --> 00:30:55,597
Children who are still awake listen to the sound passing under their windows, fading slowly down the lane, and feel safe enough to sleep.

232
00:30:57,347 --> 00:31:00,166
The lantern maker's shop is dark now.

233
00:31:00,166 --> 00:31:08,929
The lanterns under his eaves have been taken in and folded flat, and only one is left burning, low, above the door.

234
00:31:08,929 --> 00:31:14,492
The bathhouse chimney gives one last thin breath of steam and then stops.

235
00:31:14,492 --> 00:31:25,464
The gate at the end of the lane is shut, and inside his little hut the gatekeeper sits by his brazier, his chin slowly sinking toward his chest.

236
00:31:27,214 --> 00:31:38,373
On a rooftop, a cat walks along the ridge of the tiles, perfectly balanced, and then sits down at the very end of the roof to wash one paw.

237
00:31:38,373 --> 00:31:46,883
Below it, in the alley, a small dog curls up on a straw mat outside a doorway, sighs, and closes its eyes.

238
00:31:46,883 --> 00:31:51,619
Somewhere a door slides shut, softly, on its wooden groove.

239
00:31:53,369 --> 00:32:02,151
Far across the city, the soba seller under the willow is packing away his bowls into the wooden boxes of his stall.

240
00:32:02,151 --> 00:32:18,415
He lifts the whole thing onto his shoulder by its pole and sets off for home, and as he walks, the little wind chime swings and rings, faint and clear, fading away down the riverbank until there is only the river.

241
00:32:20,165 --> 00:32:26,125
And now the clappers again, very far away, at the other end of the neighbourhood.

242
00:32:26,125 --> 00:32:27,082
Clack, clack.

243
00:32:27,082 --> 00:32:28,848
And the long, soft call.

244
00:32:28,848 --> 00:32:37,899
And then, from much farther still, from another watchman in another lane, an answering clack, so faint it might be a dream.

245
00:32:37,899 --> 00:32:43,418
And over all of it, more than a million people, asleep under the same moon.

246
00:32:45,168 --> 00:32:52,663
The watchman's route takes him past the shuttered shops, along the canal, and back through the alleys.

247
00:32:52,663 --> 00:32:54,353
He knows every doorway.

248
00:32:54,353 --> 00:33:00,746
He knows which house has a new baby, and he claps a little more softly as he passes it.

249
00:33:00,746 --> 00:33:09,637
He knows which old woman likes to hear him go by before she sleeps, and he calls a little more slowly beneath her window.

250
00:33:11,387 --> 00:33:16,168
Overhead, the stars turn slowly across the sky above the roofs.

251
00:33:16,168 --> 00:33:26,183
The moon has passed over the fire watchtower and begun its long slide down toward the west, toward the castle and the far mountains.

252
00:33:26,183 --> 00:33:35,515
The hours of the night are long in autumn, and there are many of them still to come before the first grey light of morning.

253
00:33:45,265 --> 00:33:46,688
You can let go now.

254
00:33:46,688 --> 00:33:49,532
The bells will keep the hours for you.

255
00:33:49,532 --> 00:33:52,602
The watchman will walk the lanes for you.

256
00:33:52,602 --> 00:34:00,013
The gates are closed, the curtains are in, and the lamps have been turned down all across the city.

257
00:34:00,013 --> 00:34:06,001
There is nothing left to do tonight but rest, as deeply and as long as you like.

258
00:34:07,751 --> 00:34:14,668
Let your breathing slow until it is as easy as the swaying of the willow over the river.

259
00:34:14,668 --> 00:34:19,620
Let your body grow as warm and heavy as it did in the hot bath.

260
00:34:19,620 --> 00:34:31,645
And let your thoughts drift away like the pleasure boat on the Sumida, growing smaller and softer, until they are only a faint golden glow on dark water.

261
00:34:33,395 --> 00:34:39,928
Outside the paper screen, the pine shadow moves very slightly on the moonlit wall.

262
00:34:39,928 --> 00:34:44,788
The bell cricket sings its small silver song, and then rests.

263
00:34:44,788 --> 00:34:58,013
Far away, beyond the castle and the hills, the snow on Mount Fuji shines faintly under the moon, keeping watch over the sleeping city, as it has for a very long time.

264
00:34:59,763 --> 00:35:05,521
Thank you for spending this autumn night in Edo with The Quiet Archive.

265
00:35:05,521 --> 00:35:11,117
The lamp is low, the kettle is quiet, and the whole city is dreaming.

266
00:35:11,117 --> 00:35:12,009
Sleep well.

267
00:35:12,009 --> 00:35:12,820
Goodnight.
